Privately Attack Chains =E2=80=94 defensive synthesis

1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS

AC-2026-UNTRUSTED-COLLAB-CI-DEPUTY =E2=80=94 RECENT / PATCHED CASE

Canonical fingerprint: untrusted collaboration text -> privileged CI
agent -> tool-policy asymmetry -> CI secret access -> external
authority UNKNOWN.

Microsoft Security documented a cross-system path involving the Claude
Code GitHub Action: untrusted GitHub issue/PR/comment content could
influence an AI agent executing inside CI, while the agent=E2=80=99s Read
capability did not receive the same environment-secret scrubbing
applied to Bash subprocesses.

Stages: GitHub issue/PR/comment [CONFIRMED/T1] -> agent processes
attacker-controlled text [CONFIRMED/T1] -> indirect prompt instruction
influences agent [CONFIRMED/T1] -> agent invokes Read within
privileged CI context [CONFIRMED/T1] -> CI secret exposure [CONFIRMED
capability/T1] -> use secret against external service [UNKNOWN] ->
persistent/release authority [UNKNOWN].

The mechanism is tool-policy asymmetry: two tools exposed to the same
agent can have materially different secret-access semantics. Shell
sandboxing does not bound the agent if another capability can traverse
the same sensitive state.

2 MATERIAL UPDATE =E2=80=94 repository persistence becomes observed propaga=
tion

The Miasma campaign supplied stronger real-world propagation evidence
for repository/config -> later privileged developer execution.
Reporting described a compromised contributor account pushing
configuration into Microsoft=E2=80=99s Azure/durabletask repository. The fi=
les
were designed to execute credential-harvesting behavior when
developers subsequently opened the repository in Claude Code, Gemini
CLI, Cursor or VS Code. GitHub subsequently disabled 73 repositories
across four Microsoft GitHub organizations.

Updated graph: previously compromised contributor authority
[CONFIRMED/T2] -> trusted repository commit [CONFIRMED/T2] ->
agent/editor configuration persistence [CONFIRMED/T2] -> developer
opens repository [CONFIRMED intended trigger/T2] ->
credential-harvesting execution [CONFIRMED payload behavior/T2] ->
stolen credential [UNKNOWN for individual downstream developer] ->
further repository authority [UNKNOWN].

3-8 SYNTHESIS

Strong recurring cluster: weak/untrusted input -> trusted agent
surface -> hidden authority inheritance -> secondary tool/config
boundary -> credential or execution authority.

Defensive detection: correlate input provenance with authority
transition; issue/comment origin -> agent invocation -> sensitive-tool
invocation; repository checkout/open -> agent/editor startup
configuration; agent execution -> CI secret access; credential
rotation -> owner/app/trusted-publisher relationships created during
credential lifetime.

QP implication: authorization belongs on each capability invocation.
agent may run in CI must not transitively mean agent may read every
CI-readable object.

Safe lab: disposable repo, fake issue text, dummy CI secret and two
benign test tools with deliberately different access policies; test
only whether untrusted text can cause the agent to cross into the
fake-secret reader.

